// The relay's link to the control plane.
//
// Internal: no SDK is generated from this and no customer calls it. It exists
// because moderation has to *land*, and the control plane cannot make that
// happen by writing a row — someone has to close the QUIC connection of the
// person who was just banned.
//
// # Why the relay dials out
//
// The obvious arrangement is the opposite one: the control plane holds a
// registry of relays and calls the one holding the session. It is rejected for a
// deployment reason and a failure reason.
//
// Deployment: relays run in a managed instance group behind a UDP load balancer
// and the control plane runs on Cloud Run. Outbound from the group to Cloud Run
// is a URL; inbound to an individual instance is a second ingress path, a second
// certificate, and a firewall rule per instance. The direction that needs no new
// attack surface is the one the relay opens.
//
// Failure: a relay that has lost the control plane knows it immediately, because
// its own stream broke. In the other arrangement the relay learns nothing and
// keeps serving a room whose bans it can no longer receive.
//
// # What flows
//
// Up: which sessions exist and who they belong to. Down: what to do about them,
// plus the keys to verify tokens with. The relay holds no database and asks no
// questions on the media path — a session is authorised once at setup against a
// snapshot it already has, because a round trip per SUBSCRIBE would put the
// control plane between a publisher and a subscriber, which is the one thing the
// architecture forbids.

